(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[12] = {"n":12,"set":"B","level":"Intermediate","band":"B1 60% · B2 40%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"How oases form","p":["To a traveller crossing a hot, dry desert, an oasis can seem almost like a miracle: suddenly, in the middle of sand and rock, there are palm trees, gardens and pools of fresh water. Oases have supported human life in deserts for thousands of years, and many of the Arabian Peninsula's historic towns, from Al-Ahsa to Tayma, grew up around them. But how can water appear in places where it hardly ever rains?","The answer usually lies underground. Rain that falls on distant mountains or hills, sometimes hundreds of kilometres away, slowly sinks into the ground. It seeps through layers of porous rock, such as sandstone, which contain tiny spaces that can hold water like a sponge. These water-bearing layers are called aquifers. Some of the water in desert aquifers fell as rain thousands of years ago, when the climate of Arabia was much wetter than it is today.","An oasis forms where this underground water reaches the surface or comes close enough to it for plants to reach. This can happen in several ways. In some places, the land is low, for example in a depression created by wind erosion, so the ground surface cuts into the water-bearing layer. In others, cracks in the rock, called faults, allow water under pressure to rise naturally to the surface as a spring. Water from such springs may flow constantly, providing a reliable supply all year round.","Al-Ahsa in eastern Saudi Arabia is one of the largest natural oases in the world. Fed by dozens of springs, it contains more than two and a half million date palms. For centuries, farmers developed careful systems of channels to share the water fairly between families and fields. In 2018, Al-Ahsa was added to UNESCO's World Heritage List as an example of how people have adapted to life in a desert environment.","Today, however, many oases face serious problems. As cities have grown and modern farming has expanded, people have pumped huge amounts of water from aquifers using deep wells. Because desert aquifers are refilled very slowly, if at all, the water level has fallen in many places. Some springs that flowed for thousands of years have now dried up completely.","Protecting oases requires using water more wisely. Modern irrigation methods, such as drip irrigation, deliver water directly to plant roots and can reduce water use significantly. Treated waste water can also be used for farming. If these measures are adopted widely, the green islands of the desert may continue to support life for generations to come."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What question does the passage mainly answer?","o":["How can oases exist in dry deserts?","Why do people travel across deserts?","How are date palms grown?","Which desert is the largest?"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"Paragraph 1 asks how water can appear where it hardly rains.","h":"كيف توجد الواحات."},{"n":2,"s":"According to paragraph 2, where does the water in oases usually come from?","o":["rain that falls far away and moves underground","rain that falls directly on the oasis","the sea","melting snow in the desert"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Rain on distant mountains sinks into the ground.","h":"أمطار بعيدة تتسرب تحت الأرض."},{"n":3,"s":"The word 'porous' in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["very hard","full of tiny holes","very hot","completely dry"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Rock with tiny spaces that hold water like a sponge.","h":"مسامي."},{"n":4,"s":"What can be inferred about some of the water in desert aquifers?","o":["It is salty sea water.","It fell as rain last year.","It was brought by people.","It is very old."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"It fell thousands of years ago when Arabia was wetter.","h":"قديمة جدًا."},{"n":5,"s":"According to paragraph 3, how do faults help form oases?","o":["They block the flow of water.","They allow water under pressure to rise to the surface.","They create sand dunes.","They make the land higher."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Cracks let pressurized water rise as springs.","h":"تسمح للماء بالصعود."},{"n":6,"s":"Why was Al-Ahsa added to UNESCO's World Heritage List?","o":["It is the oldest city in the world.","It has the tallest palm trees.","It shows how people have adapted to desert life.","It produces the most oil."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 gives the reason.","h":"مثال على التكيف."},{"n":7,"s":"What is the purpose of paragraph 5?","o":["to explain how springs form","to describe the history of Al-Ahsa","to recommend modern irrigation","to describe a threat facing oases"],"k":3,"ty":"insertion / paragraph function","e":"It explains falling water levels from over-pumping.","h":"وصف تهديد."},{"n":8,"s":"Why has the water level fallen in many oases?","o":["The climate has become wetter.","Palm trees use less water today.","Too much water has been pumped from slowly refilled aquifers.","Earthquakes have closed the springs."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"الضخ الجائر."},{"n":9,"s":"Which sentence best paraphrases 'Desert aquifers are refilled very slowly, if at all'?","o":["Desert aquifers are filled quickly by rain.","Desert aquifers receive new water slowly or not at all.","Desert aquifers never lose water.","Desert aquifers are always full."],"k":1,"ty":"paraphrase","e":"Same meaning, different words.","h":"إعادة صياغة صحيحة."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the writer's attitude towards the future of oases?","o":["completely hopeless","cautiously hopeful","uninterested","angry"],"k":1,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"'If these measures are adopted widely … may continue'.","h":"متفائل بحذر."}]},{"t":"The history of paper money","p":["Imagine trying to buy groceries with a bag of salt, a few shells or a heavy metal bar. For most of history, people exchanged goods directly or used valuable objects as money. Paper money, which we now take for granted, was a revolutionary idea that depended on one thing above all: trust.","Before paper money, coins made of gold, silver and copper were widely used. Coins had real value because of the metal they contained, but they were heavy to carry in large amounts and attractive to thieves. In China, merchants began to look for a more convenient solution. During the Tang dynasty, around the ninth century, merchants left their heavy coins with trusted dealers and received paper receipts in return. These receipts could be exchanged for coins later, and soon people began trading the receipts themselves.","In the eleventh century, the Song dynasty government took control of this system and began printing official paper money. When the Venetian traveller Marco Polo visited China in the thirteenth century, he was astonished to see people accepting pieces of paper made from the bark of mulberry trees as payment. In his writings, he described how the emperor's money was valued throughout the empire.","However, China also experienced one of the first great dangers of paper money: inflation. Because printing more money was easy, governments sometimes printed too much to pay for wars and other expenses. As a result, the money lost its value, and prices rose sharply. Eventually, the Ming dynasty stopped using paper money for several centuries.","In Europe, paper money developed later. In the seventeenth century, banks in Sweden and England began issuing banknotes that promised to pay the holder a fixed amount of gold or silver. For a long time, many countries followed the 'gold standard', in which the value of money was linked to a fixed amount of gold. Most countries abandoned this system in the twentieth century. Today, money has value mainly because governments declare it to be legal tender and because people trust that it will be accepted.","Modern banknotes include many security features, such as watermarks, special threads and holograms, to prevent forgery. Saudi Arabia issued its first official banknotes in the 1950s, replacing earlier 'pilgrim receipts'. Today, however, cash is used less and less as people pay with cards and mobile phones. Some experts believe that paper money may eventually disappear, while others argue that cash will remain important for privacy and for people without bank accounts."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"According to the writer, what did paper money depend on most?","o":["trust","gold","salt","metal coins"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"'depended on one thing above all: trust'.","h":"الثقة."},{"n":12,"s":"What was one problem with using coins?","o":["They had no real value.","They were heavy to carry in large amounts.","They were made of paper.","They were only used in Europe."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"ثقيلة."},{"n":13,"s":"How did paper money begin in China?","o":["The emperor ordered it in the first century.","Marco Polo introduced it.","Banks in Sweden sent it to China.","Merchants received paper receipts for coins they left with dealers."],"k":3,"ty":"sequence","e":"Paragraph 2 describes the receipts.","h":"إيصالات ورقية."},{"n":14,"s":"Why was Marco Polo astonished?","o":["China had no money at all.","Coins were made of mulberry bark.","The emperor refused to pay him.","People accepted paper as payment."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains his surprise.","h":"قبول الورق كوسيلة دفع."},{"n":15,"s":"The word 'inflation' in paragraph 4 refers to","o":["a type of printing machine","a war between dynasties","a situation in which money loses value and prices rise","a kind of gold coin"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Paragraph 4 explains the effect.","h":"التضخم."},{"n":16,"s":"Why did the Ming dynasty stop using paper money?","o":["Too much money had been printed, and it lost its value.","Paper became too expensive.","Merchants preferred salt.","Marco Polo advised them to stop."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Inflation followed excessive printing.","h":"طباعة مفرطة."},{"n":17,"s":"What was the 'gold standard'?","o":["a law banning gold coins","a prize for the best banknote","a system linking money's value to a fixed amount of gold","a type of security feature"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 defines it.","h":"ربط قيمة النقد بالذهب."},{"n":18,"s":"According to paragraph 5, why does money have value today?","o":["It can be exchanged for gold.","It is made of valuable paper.","Governments declare it legal and people trust it.","It is linked to oil prices."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"إقرار الحكومة وثقة الناس."},{"n":19,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a security feature?","o":["watermarks","a microchip inside the note","special threads","holograms"],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Microchips are not mentioned.","h":"الشريحة غير مذكورة."},{"n":20,"s":"How does the writer present the future of cash?","o":["by giving two different expert views","by predicting it will disappear next year","by arguing that cards are dangerous","by describing only the advantages of cash"],"k":0,"ty":"organization","e":"Some say it will disappear; others say it will remain.","h":"عرض رأيين."}]},{"t":"How noise affects health","p":["Car horns, construction machines, aeroplanes and loud music are part of daily life in modern cities. Most people think of noise simply as an annoyance, but scientists now recognize that long-term exposure to noise can seriously affect our physical and mental health. The World Health Organization considers environmental noise one of the most important environmental risks to health, second only to air pollution in Europe.","Sound is measured in decibels (dB). A normal conversation is about sixty decibels, heavy traffic is about eighty-five, and a rock concert can reach one hundred and twenty. The decibel scale is not like an ordinary scale: an increase of ten decibels means that the sound is about twice as loud to our ears. Sounds above eighty-five decibels can damage hearing if we are exposed to them for long periods, and very loud sounds can cause damage almost immediately.","The most obvious effect of noise is hearing loss. Inside the ear, tiny hair cells turn sound vibrations into signals that the brain can understand. Loud noise can damage or destroy these cells, and unlike many other cells in the body, they do not grow back. Young people who listen to loud music through headphones for hours each day are at particular risk.","However, noise also affects the body in less obvious ways. Even when we are asleep, our bodies react to sudden sounds. The heart beats faster, and the body releases stress hormones. Over time, people who live near busy roads or airports may suffer from poor sleep, high blood pressure and a greater risk of heart disease. Some studies have also linked noise with lower concentration and poorer reading skills in children whose schools are near airports.","Noise can affect mental well-being too. Constant background noise can make people feel tense, irritable and tired, and it can make it harder to relax and enjoy time with family. Interestingly, how much noise bothers us depends partly on whether we feel we can control it. The same sound may feel much worse if it comes from a neighbour's party than from our own music.","Cities are using several strategies to reduce noise. These include building noise barriers along highways, using quieter road surfaces, planting trees, limiting night flights and setting rules for construction hours. Individuals can also protect themselves by keeping the volume of headphones below sixty per cent, using ear protection at loud events and creating quiet spaces at home."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Noise is only a minor annoyance.","Cities should ban all music.","Noise can harm both physical and mental health.","Air pollution is not important."],"k":2,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage describes many health effects of noise.","h":"الضوضاء تضر الصحة الجسدية والنفسية."},{"n":22,"s":"According to paragraph 2, what does an increase of ten decibels mean?","o":["The sound becomes ten times quieter.","The sound becomes dangerous immediately.","The sound is the same as a conversation.","The sound seems about twice as loud."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains the decibel scale.","h":"ضعف الشدة تقريبًا."},{"n":23,"s":"Why is hearing loss from noise often permanent?","o":["Damaged hair cells in the ear do not grow back.","The brain stops working.","The ear becomes blocked.","Sound vibrations remain in the ear."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"الخلايا لا تتجدد."},{"n":24,"s":"Why does the writer mention young people with headphones?","o":["to recommend expensive headphones","to show that music is good for health","to explain how ears work","as an example of a group at risk of hearing damage"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"They are 'at particular risk'.","h":"مثال على فئة معرضة."},{"n":25,"s":"The phrase 'less obvious' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["more dangerous","harder to notice","very common","easier to treat"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Effects that are not easy to see.","h":"أقل وضوحًا."},{"n":26,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as an effect of noise in paragraph 4?","o":["weaker bones","poor sleep","high blood pressure","lower concentration in children"],"k":0,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Bones are not mentioned.","h":"العظام غير مذكورة."},{"n":27,"s":"According to paragraph 5, what affects how much noise bothers us?","o":["the colour of the room","the time of year","whether we feel we can control it","how old we are"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"الشعور بالسيطرة."},{"n":28,"s":"Why might a neighbour's party feel worse than our own music?","o":["The neighbour's music is always louder.","We cannot control the neighbour's noise.","Parties last longer than songs.","We do not like our neighbours."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"Control affects how we feel about noise.","h":"لا نتحكم بها."},{"n":29,"s":"What is the purpose of the last paragraph?","o":["to describe the history of cities","to compare different countries","to explain how decibels are measured","to suggest ways of reducing noise"],"k":3,"ty":"insertion / paragraph function","e":"It lists strategies for cities and individuals.","h":"اقتراح حلول."},{"n":30,"s":"Which statement is a fact rather than an opinion?","o":["City life is unbearable.","Music should never be loud.","Neighbours are always noisy.","A normal conversation is about sixty decibels."],"k":3,"ty":"fact / opinion","e":"A measurable fact from paragraph 2.","h":"حقيقة قابلة للقياس."}]},{"t":"The spice trade of the Indian Ocean","p":["Pepper, cinnamon, cloves, nutmeg and cardamom are found in almost every kitchen today, but for much of history, they were among the most valuable goods in the world. The desire for spices shaped trade routes, built rich cities, inspired voyages of exploration and even led to wars. At the centre of this story was the Indian Ocean, a vast body of water linking Africa, Arabia, India and Southeast Asia, across which ships had carried goods and ideas for more than two thousand years.","Most of the world's important spices grew in tropical regions of southern India, Sri Lanka and the islands of Southeast Asia, especially the Moluccas, known as the Spice Islands. Cloves and nutmeg, for example, grew naturally only on a few small islands. Yet the demand for spices came from far away: from the Middle East, East Africa, the Mediterranean and later northern Europe. People used them not only to flavour food, but also in medicine, perfumes and religious ceremonies.","For centuries, Arab, Persian, Indian and Chinese merchants dominated the Indian Ocean trade. They learned to use the monsoon winds, which blow from the south-west in summer and from the north-east in winter. By timing their voyages carefully, sailors in wooden ships called dhows could cross the ocean and return within a year. Ports such as Muscat, Aden, Calicut and Malacca became wealthy, multicultural cities where traders from many lands met, exchanged goods and shared ideas.","The trade also helped to spread religions and languages. Muslim merchants carried Islam along the trade routes to East Africa, India and Southeast Asia, where many people adopted the faith peacefully through contact with traders. The Swahili language of East Africa, for instance, contains many words that come from Arabic.","In the fifteenth century, European powers began searching for a sea route to the spice-producing lands, partly to avoid paying the high prices charged by middlemen. In 1498, the Portuguese explorer Vasco da Gama reached India by sailing around Africa, reportedly with help from a local navigator who knew the Indian Ocean. Over the following centuries, the Portuguese, Dutch and British fought to control the spice trade, often using violence against local rulers and traders.","Eventually, spice plants were grown in many other tropical regions, and prices fell dramatically. Spices lost their status as luxury goods. Yet the legacy of the spice trade can still be seen today in the mixed cultures, cuisines and architecture of the ports that once connected the world."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how to cook with spices","the life of Vasco da Gama","the importance and history of the spice trade","the weather of the Indian Ocean"],"k":2,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage explains the spice trade's role in history.","h":"تاريخ تجارة التوابل."},{"n":32,"s":"According to paragraph 2, why were cloves and nutmeg especially valuable?","o":["They grew naturally only in a few places.","They were used only by kings.","They grew everywhere in Europe.","They were easy to transport."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"They grew only on a few small islands.","h":"نموها محدود."},{"n":33,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a use of spices?","o":["flavouring food","making clothes","medicine","perfumes"],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Clothes are not mentioned.","h":"الملابس غير مذكورة."},{"n":34,"s":"How did sailors use the monsoon winds?","o":["They avoided sailing during the monsoon.","They used them to grow spices.","They timed their voyages to cross and return within a year.","They used them only for fishing."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"توقيت الرحلات."},{"n":35,"s":"The word 'dominated' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["avoided","controlled","destroyed","studied"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Merchants controlled the trade.","h":"سيطروا."},{"n":36,"s":"What does the example of Swahili show?","o":["Arabic was not used in Africa.","Swahili is the oldest language.","Merchants did not travel to Africa.","Trade spread languages as well as goods."],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"Swahili contains many Arabic words.","h":"انتشار اللغات."},{"n":37,"s":"Why did European powers want a sea route to the spice lands?","o":["to avoid the high prices charged by middlemen","to spread the Swahili language","to escape the monsoon winds","to sell spices to India"],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 gives this reason.","h":"تجنب الوسطاء."},{"n":38,"s":"What does the word 'reportedly' suggest about the help da Gama received?","o":["It is a proven fact.","The writer completely disagrees with it.","It happened many times.","The information comes from reports and may not be certain."],"k":3,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"reportedly signals that it is based on accounts.","h":"حسب الروايات."},{"n":39,"s":"Why did spices lose their status as luxury goods?","o":["People stopped liking them.","Governments banned them.","They were grown in more regions, so prices fell.","Ships became too slow."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 6 explains this.","h":"زيادة الإنتاج وانخفاض الأسعار."},{"n":40,"s":"What point does the writer make in the last sentence?","o":["The spice trade still influences the culture of port cities.","The ports are no longer used.","Spices are still more valuable than gold.","Europe controls the spice trade today."],"k":0,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"Its legacy can still be seen today.","h":"الأثر باقٍ."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"She ______ the piano for ten years, and she still practises every day.","o":["is playing","plays since","played since","has been playing"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"has been playing — Duration continuing to now: present perfect continuous.","h":"المضارع التام المستمر."},{"n":42,"s":"This bridge ______ by thousands of cars every day.","o":["uses","is using","is used","used by"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"is used — Present passive.","h":"المبني للمجهول."},{"n":43,"s":"I'd rather you ______ the window. It's cold.","o":["didn't open","don't open","not open","won't open"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"didn't open — would rather + subject + past simple.","h":"would rather + الماضي."},{"n":44,"s":"The man ______ wallet I found thanked me warmly.","o":["who","which","whose","that's"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"whose — whose for possession.","h":"whose."},{"n":45,"s":"If you ______ me earlier, I could have helped you.","o":["told","had told","would tell","have told"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had told — Third conditional: if + past perfect.","h":"الشرط الثالث."},{"n":46,"s":"The young man denied ______ the shop window.","o":["to break","break","breaking","that break"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"breaking — deny + -ing.","h":"deny + -ing."},{"n":47,"s":"I need to get my eyes ______ before I renew my licence.","o":["test","tested","testing","to test"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"tested — Causative: get something + past participle.","h":"التركيب السببي."},{"n":48,"s":"The older he gets, ______ he becomes.","o":["the wiser","wiser","the wisest","more wise"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"the wiser — the + comparative, the + comparative.","h":"كلما … كلما."},{"n":49,"s":"They ______ have arrived yet. The flight was delayed by three hours.","o":["must","should to","mustn't to","can't"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"can't — Logical impossibility: can't have.","h":"استنتاج النفي."},{"n":50,"s":"If only I ______ so much time on my phone last night!","o":["didn't spend","hadn't spent","haven't spent","wouldn't spend"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"hadn't spent — Past regret: if only + past perfect.","h":"ندم على الماضي."},{"n":51,"s":"This time next year, I ______ at university.","o":["study","will be studying","am studying","will have studied"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will be studying — Action in progress at a future time.","h":"المستقبل المستمر."},{"n":52,"s":"She told me that she ______ the film the week before.","o":["has seen","sees","will see","had seen"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had seen — Reported speech: past perfect.","h":"الكلام المنقول."},{"n":53,"s":"______ he was tired, he kept working until midnight.","o":["Despite","Even though","Because","In spite"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Even though — Contrast clause: Even though.","h":"Even though."},{"n":54,"s":"There were ______ mistakes in his essay that the teacher returned it.","o":["such","too much","so much","so many"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"so many — so many + countable noun + that.","h":"so many."},{"n":55,"s":"Each of the players ______ a medal at the ceremony.","o":["were given","have been given","was given","are giving"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"was given — Each of … takes a singular verb.","h":"Each مفرد."},{"n":56,"s":"My father ______ go fishing with his friends on Fridays when he was younger.","o":["used to","is used to","was used","uses to"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"used to — Past habit: used to.","h":"used to."},{"n":57,"s":"The museum, ______ was built in 1990, has just been renovated.","o":["that","which","where","what"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"which — Non-defining clause for a thing: which.","h":"which."},{"n":58,"s":"Rarely ______ such a beautiful sunset.","o":["I have seen","I saw","seen I have","have I seen"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"have I seen — Negative adverb at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Rarely."},{"n":59,"s":"I'd prefer ______ at home tonight rather than go out.","o":["to stay","staying","stay","stayed"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to stay — would prefer + to + verb.","h":"would prefer + to."},{"n":60,"s":"Were it not for your help, I ______ the project on time.","o":["didn't finish","won't finish","wouldn't have finished","haven't finished"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"wouldn't have finished — Inverted conditional about the past.","h":"شرط مقلوب."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"The survey (A) show that (B) most residents (C) support the new (D) park.","o":["show","most","support","park"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"show — The survey is singular: shows.","h":"الفاعل مفرد: shows."},{"n":62,"s":"Ahmed (A) has been (B) learning English (C) since five years and (D) speaks it well.","o":["has","learning","since five years","speaks"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"since five years — for + a period: for five years.","h":"for مع المدة."},{"n":63,"s":"If I (A) had (B) known about the meeting, I (C) would have (D) came.","o":["had","known","would have","came"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"came — Third conditional: would have come.","h":"التصريف الثالث: come."},{"n":64,"s":"He (A) suggested (B) to go to the beach (C) after (D) lunch.","o":["suggested","to go","after","lunch"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"to go — suggest + -ing: suggested going.","h":"suggest + -ing."},{"n":65,"s":"The (A) student (B) who (C) his bag was stolen (D) reported it to the police.","o":["student","who","his","reported"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"his — Possession in a relative clause: whose bag.","h":"whose."},{"n":66,"s":"(A) Every students (B) must (C) bring their (D) ID cards.","o":["Every","must","bring","ID"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Every — Every + singular noun; with students use All.","h":"All students."},{"n":67,"s":"I wish I (A) had (B) went to the (C) concert (D) last night.","o":["had","went","concert","last"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"went — wish + past perfect: had gone.","h":"التصريف الثالث: gone."},{"n":68,"s":"It was (A) such (B) a hot day that we (C) stayed (D) at inside.","o":["such","a","stayed","at inside"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"at inside — inside needs no preposition: stayed inside.","h":"inside دون at."},{"n":69,"s":"(A) Despite it was raining, (B) the children (C) played (D) outside.","o":["Despite","the","played","outside"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Despite — A clause needs Although: Although it was raining.","h":"Although مع الجملة."},{"n":70,"s":"She (A) asked me (B) where (C) had I been (D) all day.","o":["asked","where","had I been","all"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"had I been — Reported question: where I had been.","h":"ترتيب الخبر."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M12-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"When can the student see Dr. Nouf this week?","o":["on Thursday afternoon","on Monday morning","on Wednesday morning","not at all"],"k":0,"ty":"correction","e":"She has added extra hours on Thursday afternoon.","h":"الخميس بعد الظهر."}],"t":"A professor's office hours","sc":["Situation: A student is speaking to a department secretary. (65 words)","Man: Hello. I'd like to see Dr. Nouf about my essay. When are her office hours?","Woman: She's usually available on Mondays and Wednesdays from ten to twelve. But this week she's at a conference until Wednesday.","Man: So I can't see her this week at all?","Woman: She's added extra hours on Thursday afternoon, from one to three, for students who missed her. You don't need an appointment."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M12-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"What does the woman finally choose?","o":["a new bag of the same type in a different colour","a refund","a repair of the zip","a more expensive bag"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"'I'll take the same bag in black, please.'","h":"حقيبة مماثلة بلون آخر."}],"t":"A broken zip on a bag","sc":["Situation: A customer returns to a shop with a bag. (67 words)","Woman: I bought this bag here last week, and the zip has already broken.","Man: I'm sorry about that. Do you have the receipt?","Woman: Yes, here it is. I'd prefer a new bag rather than a repair, if possible.","Man: Of course. It's still under guarantee, so you can choose any bag of the same price, or we can refund your money.","Woman: I'll take the same bag in black, please."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M12-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"How much will each person pay if five people share the cost?","o":["two hundred riyals","one hundred and twenty riyals","six hundred riyals","one hundred riyals"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Six hundred divided by five is one hundred and twenty.","h":"١٢٠ ريالًا."}],"t":"Splitting the cost of a gift","sc":["Situation: Three colleagues are buying a gift for their manager. (57 words)","Man: The watch costs six hundred riyals. If the three of us share it, that's two hundred each.","Man: That's a bit much for me this month. What if we ask Saad and Hamad to join us?","Man: Good idea. With five of us, it would be only one hundred and twenty each.","Man: That's much better. I'll message them now."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M12-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"Why was the taxi late?","o":["The driver was delayed by an accident.","The driver went to the wrong address.","The booking was cancelled.","The car broke down."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'The driver was delayed by an accident on the main road.'","h":"حادث على الطريق."}],"t":"A late taxi to the airport","sc":["Situation: A woman is calling a taxi company. (77 words)","Woman: I booked a taxi to the airport for five o'clock. It's now ten past, and it still hasn't arrived. My flight leaves at seven.","Man: I'm very sorry, madam. The driver was delayed by an accident on the main road. Another car is three minutes away.","Woman: Will I make it to the airport in time?","Man: Yes, the driver will take the ring road, which is clear at the moment. And there will be no charge for this journey."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M12-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"Why does the student want to change groups?","o":["Group A is too difficult.","His friends are in Group B.","Group B has a better teacher.","His job makes it hard to arrive on time for Group A."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"He finishes work at seven and is often late for the eight o'clock class.","h":"عمله يسبب التأخر."}],"t":"Asking to change a group","sc":["Situation: A student is speaking to his course coordinator. (72 words)","Man: Excuse me, Dr. Waleed. Is it possible to change from Group A to Group B in the English course?","Man: Why do you want to change?","Man: Group A meets at eight in the morning, and I have a part-time job that finishes at seven. I'm often late because of the traffic.","Man: Group B is nearly full, but there are two places left. Fill in this form, and I'll move you from next week."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M12-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"What does the librarian ask the student to do?","o":["leave the library immediately","put his phone on silent and take calls outside","give her his phone","switch his phone off completely"],"k":1,"ty":"function","e":"She asks him to put it on silent and take the call in the corridor.","h":"الوضع الصامت والرد خارجًا."}],"t":"A loud phone in the library","sc":["Situation: A librarian speaks quietly to a student. (55 words)","Woman: Excuse me, your phone has rung three times. Could you put it on silent, please? Other students are trying to concentrate.","Man: Oh, I'm so sorry. I'm waiting for an important call from my father.","Woman: That's fine, but please take the call in the corridor, outside the reading room.","Man: Of course. I'll switch it to vibrate."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M12-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"When must special meals be requested?","o":["at the airport","at least twenty-four hours before the flight","one week before the flight","on the plane"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"'at least twenty-four hours before the flight'.","h":"قبل ٢٤ ساعة."}],"t":"Requesting a vegetarian meal on a flight","sc":["Situation: A passenger is speaking to an airline agent on the phone. (58 words)","Man: Hello. I'm flying to Kuala Lumpur next week, and I'd like to request a vegetarian meal.","Woman: Certainly. Special meals must be requested at least twenty-four hours before the flight, so that's no problem. Could you give me your booking reference?","Man: Yes, it's K-L-seven-four-two.","Woman: Thank you. I've added a vegetarian meal for both flights, the outward and the return."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M12-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"Why hasn't the student received any emails?","o":["The course was cancelled.","Her emails went to her teacher.","The centre's system was broken.","Her email address was typed incorrectly. Longer conversations"],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"The address was missing a letter.","h":"خطأ في عنوان البريد."}],"t":"A wrong email address on a form","sc":["Situation: A student is speaking to an office worker at a language centre. (59 words)","Woman: I registered for the summer course last week, but I haven't received any emails.","Man: Let me check your form. Ah, I see the problem. Your email address is missing a letter. It says 'gmai' instead of 'gmail'.","Woman: Oh, I must have typed it too quickly.","Man: No problem. I've corrected it, and I'll send you the welcome information again now."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M12-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"Why don't they sell cakes?","o":["Cakes are too expensive to make.","The school does not allow food.","Other classes are already selling them.","They cannot bake."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"'three other classes are already selling cakes'.","h":"صفوف أخرى تبيعها."},{"n":80,"s":"Why does the second student want to make a poster?","o":["People donate more when they understand the cause.","The teacher asked for a poster.","Posters are cheaper than bracelets.","She wants to win a design prize."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'People give more when they understand the cause.'","h":"يتبرع الناس أكثر عند الفهم."}],"t":"Planning a fundraising stall","sc":["Situation: Two students are planning a stall at the school fair. (144 words)","Woman: We need to raise money for the children's cancer charity. What should we sell at our stall?","Woman: Food always sells well. We could sell homemade cakes and cookies.","Woman: Yes, but three other classes are already selling cakes. We need something different.","Woman: What about handmade bookmarks and bracelets? We could make them in our art lessons.","Woman: That's original. And they're cheap to make, so most of the money will go to the charity.","Woman: How much should we charge?","Woman: Ten riyals for a bracelet and five for a bookmark. And we could have a jar for extra donations.","Woman: I'll design a poster explaining what the charity does. People give more when they understand the cause.","Woman: Great. I'll ask Ms. Reem if we can use the art room after school. If we start this week, we should have at least a hundred bracelets ready before the fair."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M12-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"According to the survey, what was the main reason for students' lack of sleep?","o":["homework","noisy homes","using phones in bed","early school start times"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'The main reason they gave was using their phones in bed.'","h":"الهواتف في السرير."},{"n":82,"s":"Why can't the students say that less sleep causes lower grades?","o":["Most students refused to answer.","Their survey did not collect information about grades.","The teacher did not allow it.","All students had the same grades."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"'Our survey didn't ask about grades'.","h":"لم يسألوا عن الدرجات."}],"t":"Discussing results of a class survey","sc":["Situation: Two students are discussing a survey they carried out. (144 words)","Man: So, we asked eighty students from Grades 10 to 12 about their sleep habits. What do the results show?","Man: The most surprising thing is that more than half of them sleep less than seven hours on school nights.","Man: That's worrying. Did we find out why?","Man: The main reason they gave was using their phones in bed. Homework was second.","Man: And did the students who slept less have lower grades?","Man: We can't say that for sure. Our survey didn't ask about grades, and eighty students is quite a small sample.","Man: True. We should mention that in our conclusion. Maybe we can recommend that the school runs a larger study next year.","Man: Good. And let's suggest a campaign to encourage students to switch off their phones an hour before bed. We could design posters and ask the head teacher to mention it in the morning assembly."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M12-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"What does the young woman want to do after graduating?","o":["work in a university laboratory","teach English","work as an engineer on construction projects","start her own company"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'I'd like to work as an engineer on construction projects'.","h":"مهندسة في مشاريع البناء."},{"n":84,"s":"Why does her brother think the London internship is less important?","o":["London is too expensive to visit.","The university is not famous.","Experience in her future field matters more.","Research jobs are not well paid. Talks and lectures"],"k":2,"ty":"opinion","e":"'Experience in your future field will matter more to employers.'","h":"الخبرة في المجال أهم."}],"t":"Choosing an internship abroad","sc":["Situation: A university student is talking to her older brother. (142 words)","Woman: I've been offered two summer internships, one in Dubai and one in London. I can't decide.","Man: What kind of work would you do in each?","Woman: In Dubai, I'd be working for a large engineering company on real building projects. In London, it's a research internship at a university laboratory.","Man: What do you want to do after you graduate?","Woman: I'd like to work as an engineer on construction projects, not in research.","Man: Then Dubai sounds more relevant. Also, it's closer, and the cost of living is lower than in London.","Woman: But London would improve my English more.","Man: Your English is already excellent. Experience in your future field will matter more to employers.","Woman: You're right. I'll accept the Dubai offer tomorrow and thank the university in London politely. Maybe I can apply to them again for a master's degree later.","Man: That's a sensible plan."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M12-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"How do geographers define a desert?","o":["by the amount of sand","by high temperatures","by the number of animals","by its low rainfall"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'geographers define a desert by its lack of rainfall'.","h":"قلة الأمطار."},{"n":86,"s":"Why is Antarctica considered a desert?","o":["It receives very little precipitation.","It is covered in sand.","It is very hot in summer.","It has no mountains."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'because it receives so little precipitation'.","h":"قلة الهطول."},{"n":87,"s":"What causes the rain shadow effect?","o":["Cold ocean currents cool the air.","Air sinks at the equator.","Sand reflects the sunlight.","Mountains force moist air to drop its rain on one side."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"The air rises, cools and rains on the windward side.","h":"الجبال تسقط المطر على جانب واحد."}],"t":"Lecture: how deserts are formed","sc":["Situation: A geography lecturer is speaking to university students. (251 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Today's topic is deserts. Many people think a desert is simply a place full of sand, but geographers define a desert by its lack of rainfall: usually less than two hundred and fifty millimetres a year. In fact, only about twenty per cent of the world's deserts are covered in sand. Others are rocky or stony, and Antarctica is technically the largest desert of all, because it receives so little precipitation. So why do deserts form? There are several main causes. The first is global air circulation. Near the equator, warm, moist air rises and loses its moisture as rain. This air then moves towards the north and south, and at around thirty degrees latitude, it sinks. As it sinks, it warms and becomes very dry, creating hot deserts such as the Sahara and the Arabian Desert. The second cause is the rain shadow effect. When moist winds meet a mountain range, the air rises, cools and drops its rain on the side facing the wind. By the time the air crosses the mountains, it is dry, leaving the land behind them with little rain. A third cause is distance from the sea: areas deep inside large continents, such as the Gobi Desert in Asia, receive little moisture because winds lose their water before arriving. Finally, cold ocean currents along some coasts cool the air above them, which reduces evaporation and rainfall. This explains coastal deserts such as the Atacama in Chile, one of the driest places on Earth."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M12-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"According to the speaker, what impresses interviewers?","o":["candidates who talk about salary first","candidates who can explain why they want to join that company","candidates who memorize their answers","candidates who arrive exactly on time"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"'Interviewers are always impressed by candidates who can explain why they want to work for that particular company.'","h":"معرفة سبب اختيار الشركة."},{"n":89,"s":"Why does the speaker advise against memorizing answers word for word?","o":["Interviewers ask different questions.","It takes too much time.","It is not allowed.","Candidates may sound unnatural."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"'or you'll sound unnatural'.","h":"يبدو الكلام غير طبيعي."},{"n":90,"s":"What does the speaker suggest doing after the interview?","o":["calling to ask about the salary","visiting the company again","sending a short thank-you email","applying for another job immediately"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'send a short email to thank the interviewer'.","h":"رسالة شكر."}],"t":"Talk: tips for job interviews","sc":["Situation: A careers advisor is speaking to final-year university students. (236 words)","Lecturer: Good afternoon, everyone. Many of you will have your first job interviews in the coming months, so today I'd like to share some practical advice. First, preparation is everything. Before the interview, research the company carefully: read its website, look at recent news and try to understand what it does and what it values. Interviewers are always impressed by candidates who can explain why they want to work for that particular company. Second, prepare examples. Most interviewers now ask questions like 'Tell me about a time when you solved a problem.' A useful method is to describe the situation, the task, the action you took and the result. Practise your answers aloud, but don't memorize them word for word, or you'll sound unnatural. Third, pay attention to body language. Arrive about ten minutes early, dress appropriately, make eye contact and listen carefully to each question before answering. If you don't understand a question, it's perfectly acceptable to ask the interviewer to repeat it. Fourth, prepare one or two questions of your own to ask at the end. Asking about training opportunities or the challenges of the role shows real interest. Avoid asking about salary in the first interview unless the interviewer raises it. Finally, after the interview, send a short email to thank the interviewer for their time. It's a small step, but many candidates forget it, and it can leave a positive final impression."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["These efforts show the Kingdom's commitment to a cleaner energy future.","Solar panels work best in sunny places.","Wind is caused by differences in air pressure.","Electricity prices change every year."],"x":["Saudi Arabia has invested heavily in renewable energy in recent years. Large solar farms have been built in the north, and new wind projects are being developed. The country aims to produce half of its electricity from renewable sources by 2030. ______"],"k":0,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"A conclusion that sums up the efforts.","h":"خلاصة الجهود."},{"n":92,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 1","Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4"],"x":["(1) Learning a second language has many cognitive benefits. (2) It improves memory and problem-solving skills. (3) Bilingual people often find it easier to focus on tasks. (4) English is the most widely studied language in the world. (5) Some studies suggest it may even delay memory loss in old age."],"k":3,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"The popularity of English does not support the cognitive benefits.","h":"جملة عن الإنجليزية خارج الفكرة."},{"n":93,"s":"Neither the manager (A) nor his assistants (B) was (C) informed about the (D) changes.","o":["nor","was","informed","changes"],"k":1,"ty":"error","e":"With neither … nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject (assistants): were.","h":"المطابقة مع الأقرب: were."},{"n":94,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 3 – 1 – 4","2 – 1 – 3 – 4","3 – 2 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 4 – 1"],"x":["(1) Consequently, many farmers have switched to drip irrigation.","(2) Water shortages are a serious problem in many dry regions.","(3) Traditional flood irrigation wastes large amounts of water through evaporation.","(4) This method delivers water directly to the roots of each plant."],"k":0,"ty":"best order","e":"problem → cause → Consequently → This method explained.","h":"مشكلة ثم سبب ثم نتيجة ثم شرح."},{"n":95,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["It is therefore important to use social media wisely and critically.","Most people check their phones every morning.","Some businesses are very old.","News programmes are shown on television."],"x":["Social media allows people to stay in touch with friends and family around the world. It also helps businesses reach new customers and gives people access to news instantly. However, it can spread false information and reduce face-to-face communication. ______"],"k":0,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"A balanced conclusion.","h":"خلاصة متوازنة."},{"n":96,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 1","Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4"],"x":["(1) Coral reefs are among the most diverse ecosystems on Earth. (2) They provide homes for about a quarter of all marine species. (3) Reefs also protect coastlines from storms and waves. (4) Many tourists enjoy diving holidays in the Red Sea. (5) Unfortunately, rising sea temperatures are causing widespread coral bleaching."],"k":3,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Diving holidays do not develop the ecological importance of reefs.","h":"السياحة خارج الفكرة."},{"n":97,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Drones are used by many photographers.","Fertilizer can be expensive.","Technology is helping farmers work more efficiently.","Harvest time depends on the weather."],"x":["______ For example, they can monitor the health of crops using drones, apply exactly the right amount of fertilizer and predict the best time to harvest. This reduces waste and increases production."],"k":2,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details are examples of farming technology.","h":"التقنية في الزراعة."},{"n":98,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["Nevertheless","Likewise","Accordingly","On the contrary"],"x":["The company's profits fell sharply last year. ______, it decided not to hire any new employees."],"k":2,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"The decision is a result of falling profits.","h":"النتيجة."},{"n":99,"s":"The (A) researchers (B) who conducted the study (C) were surprised (D) from the results.","o":["researchers","who","were","from"],"k":3,"ty":"error","e":"surprised by / at, not from.","h":"surprised by."},{"n":100,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Many shops are located in malls.","It offers customers convenience, lower prices and a wider choice of products.","Delivery drivers work long hours.","Some people prefer to pay in cash. Teacher Section — Mock 12"],"x":["Online shopping has grown rapidly in recent years. ______ However, it has also made life more difficult for many small local shops."],"k":1,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"Benefits before 'However' introduces a drawback.","h":"فوائد قبل However."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
